</tbody></table></div>

<h2 id="after-write">Freshness after a write</h2>
<p>Once Mina’s purchase has been confirmed, her next page needs the resulting order. A fresh current transaction started after that confirmation can read the committed result. A historical snapshot from before the purchase cannot, however safely a nearby follower serves it.</p>
<p>“At most ten seconds old” does not mean “includes my write”. If the purchase committed one second ago, a permitted five-second-old snapshot precedes it. A minimum-history bound can express a dependency when the application has a valid timestamp covering that write, but choosing and propagating that bound is part of the protocol. Starting a fresh current transaction is the direct choice when the next read requires the newly confirmed state.</p>
<p>If the purchase’s outcome is uncertain, an absent row in a historical result proves only that the row was absent at that earlier time. It cannot establish that the purchase failed. Recover the protected request identity, such as <code>purchase-103</code>, through <a href="/learn/databases/relational/cockroachdb/transactions/">CockroachDB transactions and retries</a>. Do not turn historical absence into permission to create another purchase.</p>

<h2 id="retention">Retained history</h2>
<p>A safe timestamp must also be available in storage. CockroachDB garbage-collects old versions, controlled by <code>gc.ttlseconds</code> in replication zone configuration. A request can be old enough for follower service and too old for the history that remains.</p>
<Sql label="Inspect the retention inherited by products" code={`SHOW ZONE CONFIGURATION FROM TABLE products;`} />
<p>Read the returned target as well as <code>gc.ttlseconds</code>. A table can inherit its settings; the answer may name the parent zone rather than the table itself. Inspect the relevant tables, indexes and any more specific configurations used by the read, rather than assuming one cluster-wide value.</p>
<p>A fixed report snapshot gets older for as long as the report runs. Budget its starting age, execution time, pauses and any need to resume against the retained-history window. Do not assume an open historical transaction grants unlimited retention. If the required history is unavailable, fail or restart the whole report at a new agreed timestamp; silently switching just the next query would mix histories.</p>
<p>Keeping versions longer costs storage. Increasing retention cannot restore versions already removed. It also does not create permanent business audit history or a recoverable copy after data loss. Those needs belong to an explicit history model and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/cockroachdb/backup-and-restore/">CockroachDB backup and recovery</a>.</p>

<h2 id="checking">Checking the read that actually ran</h2>
<p>Successful SQL establishes that CockroachDB returned an answer, but does not establish that a follower served it. Run <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code> on the read whose path matters. It executes the statement, so use a representative, bounded query.</p>
<Sql label="Inspect an executed bounded-staleness lookup" code={`EXPLAIN ANALYZE
SELECT price
FROM products AS OF SYSTEM TIME with_max_staleness('10s')
WHERE product_id = 'P7';`} />
<p>The <code>historical</code> field reports the chosen AS OF timestamp and whether bounded staleness was used. The plan’s <code>sql nodes</code> and <code>kv nodes</code> distinguish SQL processing from the nodes supplying the data; <code>regions</code> adds locality when available. <code>used follower read</code> is the query-level evidence for follower service. <code>distribution: local</code> alone is not that evidence.</p>
<p>The SQL examples and two-session schedules here were checked on owned, disposable single-node CockroachDB v26.2.8 fixtures. The executed bounded lookup reported a historical timestamp and SQL/KV node n1, with no <code>used follower read</code> annotation. It verifies historical SQL and isolation behaviour. It supplies no observation of a remote leaseholder, nearby follower, regional latency or failure availability.</p>
<p>On the real topology, check the effective isolation, the actual timestamp and the serving path separately. Then compare them with the decision the answer will support. A safe follower can answer the past correctly; the application must still decide whether that past is recent enough for its next action.</p>

